3. Corrected Integration Ownership

A critical correction has been made here.

3I must not become the universal authority that declares every subsystem internally correct.

Each subsystem must validate its own mechanisms.

The architecture is:

LOCAL VALIDATION

3A validates runtime/contracts
        ↓
3B validates cognition
        ↓
3C validates work/orchestration
        ↓
3D validates resources
        ↓
3E validates memory
        ↓
3F validates verification/research
        ↓
3G validates learning/adaptation

        ↓

3I CROSS-LAYER INTEGRATION

        ↓

Check:

Authority boundaries
Data-flow boundaries
State ownership
Event consistency
Failure isolation
Security invariants
Financial boundary
Human-control boundary
Version compatibility
Deployment compatibility

        ↓

ACCEPT / REJECT / OPEN

Therefore:

> Subsystems prove their local correctness. 3I checks whether they operate safely together.



3I owns integration correctness, not the internal correctness of every subsystem.


---

4. Security Architecture

Security is a capability composed of multiple mechanisms and resources, not one security model.

Possible security resources include:

static analysis

dependency analysis

vulnerability intelligence

runtime monitoring

authorization

audit controls

security workflows

security scanners

specialized security reasoning resources

configuration integrity checks

filesystem/code integrity checks

network/security monitoring


No single Security LLM is the whole security system.

Security mechanisms include:

identity

authentication

authorization

least privilege

authority levels

scoped permissions

context-bound authority

prompt/instruction integrity

prompt-injection resistance

tool authorization

model/resource isolation

data classification

data-flow control

secret management

secret leak prevention

memory security

user/account isolation

environment isolation

sandboxing

blast-radius control

plugin/extension security

supply-chain trust

dependency trust

composed-authority analysis

privilege-escalation detection



---

5. Authority Model

Authority must be explicit and scoped.

No:

model

agent

bot

tool

plugin

resource

external service


may grant itself authority.

A conceptual authority structure is:

AuthorityContext {
    principal
    role
    scopes[]
    work_id
    task_id
    purpose
    expiry
    policy_version
}

Authority evaluation:

validate(
    action,
    context,
    resource,
    state
)
        ↓
ALLOW
DENY
ESCALATE

Authority should consider:

who is requesting

what they are requesting

why

which task it belongs to

what resource is being used

what data is involved

what side effects exist

current system state

applicable policy

authority scope

expiry

consequence



---

6. Human Control

Human authority remains fundamental.

However, human control must be technically meaningful rather than merely a button saying “approve.”

For actions exceeding configured autonomous authority:

ACTION REQUEST
      ↓
AUTHORITY CHECK
      ↓
INSUFFICIENT AUTHORITY
      ↓
HUMAN ESCALATION
      ↓
HUMAN DECISION
      ↓
AUTHORITY VALIDATION
      ↓
ACTION / DENIAL

An escalation should contain:

Escalation {
    reason
    blocked_action
    evidence_refs[]
    uncertainty
    policy_rule
    alternatives[]
    recommended_next_step
    expiry
}

The human should understand:

what CNS wants to do

why

what evidence supports it

what uncertainty exists

what alternatives exist

what happens if nothing is done

what authority is required


Critical boundary

> Human authority is highest within the permitted operational boundary, but a human command does not authorize violation of immutable CNS safety/security constraints.



This prevents the dangerous interpretation that:

> “Human authority means the user can bypass everything.”



They cannot.


---

7. Financial Boundary

3I and 3G have deliberately separate responsibilities.

3I owns:

security

authorization

identity

permission

data protection

security blocking

authority around financial operations


3G owns:

financial safety

financial state

risk controls

order validation

execution

reconciliation

financial failure handling


Therefore:

3I
SECURITY + AUTHORITY
       ↓
3G
FINANCIAL SAFETY + EXECUTION

3I cannot replace 3G financial mechanisms.

3G cannot bypass 3I security and authority controls.


---

8. Security Failure Isolation and Containment

This is another important correction.

A security failure must not automatically mean that the entire CNS shuts down.

First determine:

what failed

where it failed

what authority was exposed

what data was exposed

what resources were affected

what dependencies exist

what the blast radius is


The preferred response is:

DETECT
  ↓
CLASSIFY
  ↓
DETERMINE BLAST RADIUS
  ↓
ISOLATE AFFECTED RESOURCE / BOUNDARY
  ↓
PRESERVE EVIDENCE
  ↓
REVOKE / REDUCE AUTHORITY IF REQUIRED
  ↓
CONTINUE SAFE UNRELATED OPERATION WHEN POSSIBLE
  ↓
RECOVER / PATCH / REPLACE
  ↓
VERIFY
  ↓
RESTORE AUTHORITY

This follows the CNS constitutional principle:

> Protection without paralysis.



However, if a critical security or safety invariant is compromised:

CRITICAL INVARIANT COMPROMISED
        ↓
SAFE STATE
        ↓
FAIL-CLOSED WHERE REQUIRED
        ↓
HUMAN ESCALATION WHERE REQUIRED

The system therefore does not:

blindly continue, or

unnecessarily destroy the entire ecosystem.


It contains the problem according to its actual blast radius.


---

9. Security Observability and Provenance

Security-sensitive operations must be observable and attributable.

Relevant information includes:

operation start

operation completion

principal

authority context

resource

resource version

input references

version references

policy version

execution metrics

outputs

evidence references

verification result

state transition

retries

failures

human interventions

stale-data events

security events

incident identifiers

authority changes

deployment decisions


Important security events must become durable CNS evidence according to the applicable event/state contracts.


---

10. Plugin / Extension Security

A plugin or extension is a controlled resource/provider mechanism.

It is not another CNS brain.

Extension path:

EXTENSION
   ↓
REGISTER
   ↓
CONTRACT VALIDATION
   ↓
CAPABILITY VALIDATION
   ↓
SECURITY / AUTHORITY CHECK
   ↓
HEALTH
   ↓
BENCHMARK
   ↓
ACTIVATE
   ↓
MONITOR
   ↓
DISABLE / REPLACE

A plugin cannot acquire additional authority merely by declaring additional capabilities.

Authority must be independently granted by CNS policy.


---

11. Model and Tool Security

Models are resources.

Their outputs are not automatically trusted.

Therefore:

MODEL OUTPUT
     ↓
INTERPRET
     ↓
VALIDATE
     ↓
AUTHORIZE IF ACTION REQUIRED
     ↓
EXECUTE

Important rules:

model output cannot redefine CNS policy

prompts cannot silently redefine authority

external instructions cannot elevate privilege

tool calls require independent authorization

tool side effects must obey their contract

sensitive information is exposed only when permitted

model reasoning cannot directly deploy privileged changes

changing the model must not change CNS authority



---

12. Prompt / Instruction Integrity

CNS must distinguish between:

SYSTEM / CONSTITUTIONAL POLICY
        ↓
GOVERNANCE POLICY
        ↓
AUTHORIZED TASK
        ↓
RESOURCE INSTRUCTIONS
        ↓
UNTRUSTED EXTERNAL CONTENT

External content can provide information.

It cannot automatically become authority.

Examples include:

web pages

documents

emails

market reports

tool output

retrieved memory

model-generated text

plugin output


A malicious instruction embedded inside such content must not silently become a CNS command.


---

13. Data Classification and Flow Control

Data should be classified according to sensitivity and permitted use.

Conceptually:

DATA
 ↓
CLASSIFY
 ↓
CHECK PURPOSE
 ↓
CHECK AUTHORITY
 ↓
CHECK DESTINATION
 ↓
ALLOW / TRANSFORM / DENY

This applies to:

user data

financial information

credentials

secrets

memory

provider information

proprietary research

model prompts

tool outputs

logs

security evidence



---

14. Change and Deployment Governance

CNS must not allow a reasoning resource to modify itself directly in production.

Change authority remains controlled.

Relevant relationship:

3G
LEARNING / ADAPTATION
       ↓
PROPOSED CHANGE
       ↓
3F WHERE RESEARCH / VERIFICATION IS REQUIRED
       ↓
3I SECURITY + AUTHORITY + DEPLOYMENT GOVERNANCE
       ↓
CONTROLLED DEPLOYMENT

Deployment flow:

NEW VERSION
      ↓
CONTROLLED EXPOSURE
      ↓
COMPARE
      ↓
SAFETY / AUTHORITY / PERFORMANCE CHECK
      ↓
EXPAND / HOLD / ROLLBACK

A failed candidate cannot silently become the active implementation.


---

15. Deployment Classes

RESEARCH

Untrusted experimentation.

No production authority.

SHADOW

Observe behavior without meaningful side effects.

CANARY

Controlled exposure.

Strict monitoring.

Limited authority.

Rollback available.

PRODUCTION

Approved operational version.

Continuous monitoring.

CRITICAL TRADING

Strictest environment.

Deterministic safeguards.

Financial safety controls.

Execution-state reconciliation.

No dependency on LLM correctness or availability.


---

16. Cross-Layer Integration Contract

3I must verify system-wide invariants.

Authority

No subsystem can silently acquire another subsystem's authority.

State

Authoritative state must have a defined owner.

Security

Security-sensitive state changes must be attributable and auditable.

Data

Data crossing trust boundaries must be classified and policy-checked.

Financial safety

Financial safety controls cannot be bypassed through intelligence resources.

LLM independence

LLM/model availability cannot become a hidden dependency of the critical trading safety path.

Provider replacement

Replacing a resource/provider cannot silently change:

authority

historical truth

governance

safety constraints


Failure isolation

Failure in one resource must not silently corrupt unrelated resources.

Version compatibility

Incompatible versions must be detected before activation.

Human approval

Human approval must be:

authentic

attributable

policy-valid

applicable to the requested action

within its validity period


Security evidence

Security incidents must preserve sufficient evidence for investigation and learning.

Event integrity

Required cross-layer events must remain replayable/reconcilable where applicable.


---

17. Cross-Layer Integration Process

The integration process is:

LOCAL CONTRACT / UNIT VALIDATION
        ↓
INTEGRATION TEST
        ↓
FAILURE DRILL
        ↓
SECURITY TEST
        ↓
AUTHORITY TEST
        ↓
PERFORMANCE TEST
        ↓
REPLAY / REPRODUCIBILITY
        ↓
CROSS-LAYER INVARIANT CHECK
        ↓
CANARY WHERE APPLICABLE
        ↓
ACCEPT / REJECT / OPEN

OPEN

means:

> Evidence is insufficient.



It does not mean:

> “We don't know, but deploy anyway.”




---

18. Failure and Incident Response

Security incident response:

DETECT
 ↓
CLASSIFY
 ↓
CONTAIN
 ↓
PRESERVE EVIDENCE
 ↓
REVOKE / REDUCE AUTHORITY
 ↓
RECOVER / REPLACE
 ↓
VERIFY
 ↓
RESTORE
 ↓
RECORD LESSON

Recovery execution remains owned by the responsible subsystem.

3I governs the security and authority conditions surrounding recovery.

3I does not steal recovery ownership from the responsible subsystem.

The result is also available for learning through the appropriate 3G mechanisms.


---

19. Acceptance Tests — INT-001 to INT-042

INT-001

Protected operations require authentication.

INT-002

Unauthorized actions are denied.

INT-003

Insufficient authority escalates rather than silently executing.

INT-004

A worker cannot grant itself authority.

INT-005

Model output cannot override policy.

INT-006

Tool calls require independent authorization.

INT-007

Prompt injection cannot redefine authority.

INT-008

Secret exposure is blocked by data-flow policy.

INT-009

Sensitive memory access respects scope.

INT-010

User/account isolation is enforced.

INT-011

Environment isolation prevents cross-environment contamination.

INT-012

An invalid plugin contract is rejected.

INT-013

An unauthorized plugin capability claim is rejected.

INT-014

An extension cannot escalate privilege after activation.

INT-015

Security/dependency failure produces appropriate quarantine.

INT-016

Security events are durably attributable.

INT-017

The audit trail survives restart.

INT-018

Human approval is attributable and policy-valid.

INT-019

Expired approval is rejected.

INT-020

Duplicate/fake approval cannot authorize another action.

INT-021

Emergency stop blocks applicable side effects.

INT-022

Security incidents preserve evidence.

INT-023

An affected resource can be isolated without unnecessary ecosystem shutdown.

INT-024

Critical invariant compromise enters a safe state.

INT-025

Unaffected safe operations can continue after a localized security failure when appropriate.

INT-026

Financial action cannot bypass 3G financial safety controls.

INT-027

A 3I security block cannot corrupt 3G financial state.

INT-028

Model-provider replacement preserves authority policy.

INT-029

Resource replacement preserves security classification.

INT-030

Version-incompatible components are blocked before activation.

INT-031

Cross-layer state-ownership conflicts are detected.

INT-032

Unauthorized state mutation is detected.

INT-033

Event/replay inconsistency is detected.

INT-034

Failure in one subsystem does not silently grant authority elsewhere.

INT-035

Composed permissions are evaluated for privilege escalation.

INT-036

A deployment candidate cannot skip a required security gate.

INT-037

Rollback revokes candidate authority where required.

INT-038

Canary expansion requires safety, authority and performance checks.

INT-039

Local subsystem validation remains distinct from 3I integration acceptance.

INT-040

Cross-layer integration failure returns REJECT or OPEN, never silent success.

INT-041

Full-system integration preserves human-control invariants.

INT-042

The final integration gate is reproducible, auditable and versioned.


