22. Cross-Log Boundary Map

3A

Provides runtime/state/event substrate.

3I governs security and authority around it.

3B

Provides cognition.

3I ensures reasoning resources cannot self-authorize.

3C

Provides work control.

3I checks worker authority and trust-boundary crossings.

3D

Owns resource lifecycle.

3I governs security qualification and authority.

3E

Owns memory.

3I protects access, provenance and sensitive-data boundaries.

3F

Owns verification/research.

3I governs security and integration constraints surrounding deployment.

3G

Owns learning/adaptation/recovery learning.

3I governs security and change authority.

Financial operating layer

Owns financial safety and execution.

3I owns security/authorization surrounding those operations.


---

23. Explicit Non-Assumptions

3I does not assume:

3I is a universal verifier.

3I is a second brain.

Security is one Security LLM.

Human control means unrestricted bypass of immutable safety/security constraints.

Every security incident requires whole-system shutdown.

More security components automatically mean better security.

A plugin is trusted merely because it is installed.

Local tests prove cross-layer correctness.

Cross-layer acceptance replaces subsystem acceptance.

Security policy can be safely hard-coded forever.

Every security decision requires an LLM.

Every security event requires human intervention.

Every failure should trigger a global restart.


Deterministic controls remain preferred where sufficient.


---

24. Final Architecture Statement

CNS remains one coherent governing ecosystem.

3I protects its boundaries, governs authority, protects people/data/resources, handles security governance, preserves human control, and checks that independently validated subsystems can operate together without violating system-wide invariants.

The corrected core principle is:

> SUBSYSTEMS OWN THEIR MECHANISMS.
3I GOVERNS THEIR SAFE INTERACTION.



This preserves:

modular ownership

authority isolation

security

human control

financial safety

replaceability

failure containment

future expansion

whole-system integration


without turning 3I into a hidden second CNS.


---

25. Final Checker Result

The previous 3I builder version contained a real boundary weakness:

> Its “final integration” responsibility could be interpreted as making 3I the universal correctness authority for every subsystem.



That was corrected.

The corrected version explicitly establishes:

LOCAL CORRECTNESS
       ↓
OWNED BY EACH SUBSYSTEM
       ↓
3I
       ↓
CROSS-LAYER SAFETY / AUTHORITY / SECURITY / INTEGRATION
       ↓
FINAL INTEGRATION GATE

A second issue was clarified:

SECURITY FAILURE
≠
AUTOMATIC TOTAL CNS SHUTDOWN

Instead:

DETECT
→ CLASSIFY
→ CONTAIN
→ PRESERVE EVIDENCE
→ RECOVER
→ VERIFY
→ RESTORE

unless a critical invariant requires a safe/failed-closed state.

A third issue was clarified:

HUMAN AUTHORITY
≠
UNRESTRICTED SAFETY BYPASS

Human authority remains fundamental, but immutable safety/security boundaries remain enforced.


